WEBVTT

history-onset
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:10.000
Ask when the vision changed and whether it has worsened gradually or suddenly.

history-symptoms
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:19.000
Ask about pain, redness, one or both eyes, and difficulty with everyday activities.

history-gradual
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:28.000
Cataract usually causes gradual, painless blurring of vision.

history-both
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:37.000
It may affect both eyes, but the symptoms can be different in each eye.

history-sudden
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:46.000
Sudden loss of vision is not the usual cataract history and needs urgent assessment.

history-pain
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:55.000
Pain or a red eye also suggests another cause. Arrange referral rather than assuming cataract.

acuity-distance
00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Measure distance vision in each eye separately, using the patient's usual distance glasses.

acuity-near
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:15.000
Check near vision and repeat reduced distance vision with a pinhole.

acuity-reduced
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:25.000
Cataract can reduce distance and near vision, as shown by the downward arrows.

acuity-pinhole
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:35.000
Vision may remain reduced through a pinhole. This supports further examination, but does not diagnose cataract by itself.

acuity-improves
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Improvement with a pinhole suggests that a refractive error contributes to the reduced vision.

acuity-refraction
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:53.000
Arrange refraction and assess the remaining findings. Cataract and refractive error can occur together.

front-examine
00:01:53.000 --> 00:02:01.000
Use the Arclight to inspect the front of each eye with its examination light.

front-compare
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:09.000
Compare the eyes and look for redness, corneal clouding and other visible abnormalities.

front-quiet
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:17.000
The front of the eye may look quiet, without redness or an obvious surface abnormality.

front-not-excluded
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:25.000
A normal-looking front of eye does not exclude a cataract behind the pupil.

front-opacity
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:34.000
A visible opacity or another abnormal appearance needs further eye assessment.

front-red
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:43.000
A red or painful eye is not explained by an uncomplicated cataract. Assess and refer appropriately.

pupil-direct
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:52.000
Shine the light into each eye in turn and observe the direct and consensual pupil responses.

pupil-swing
00:02:52.000 --> 00:03:01.000
Compare the responses as the light moves between the eyes.

pupil-constrict
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:09.000
In the illustrated normal responses, the pupils become smaller when light is shone into the eyes.

pupil-cataract
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:17.000
Normal pupil reactions can be present in a patient with cataract.

pupil-abnormal
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:26.000
A weak, absent or unequal pupil response needs further assessment.

pupil-rapd
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:35.000
An afferent pupil defect suggests disease beyond a simple cataract. Arrange referral.

reflex-position
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:44.000
In a dim room, look through the Arclight from a distance and compare the fundal reflexes.

reflex-observe
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:53.000
Look for the brightness, colour and any shadows within each pupil.

reflex-opacity
00:03:53.000 --> 00:04:04.000
This green-column example is an exception: it shows lens opacity and dark interruptions consistent with cataract, not normal findings.

reflex-patterns
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:15.000
The appearance varies with the position and density of the opacity. Assess it together with the history and vision.

reflex-white
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:25.000
A white, unusually pale, absent or asymmetric reflex must not automatically be labelled cataract.

reflex-referral
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:35.000
Other eye disease may cause an abnormal reflex. Arrange eye assessment; a white pupil in a child needs urgent referral.

fundus-close
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:44.000
Move closer for fundoscopy, keeping the Arclight aligned with the pupil.

fundus-view
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:53.000
Assess the view through the lens and inspect the optic disc and retina when visible.

fundus-hazy
00:04:53.000 --> 00:05:02.000
Lens opacity can make the fundus look hazy or prevent a clear view.

fundus-limits
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:11.000
An obscured fundus does not prove that the retina is healthy. Consider specialist assessment.

fundus-abnormal
00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:21.000
Visible abnormalities of the optic disc or retina may explain vision loss and require referral.

fundus-combine
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:31.000
Combine all six parts of the examination. Cataract can coexist with other disease, so refer according to the complete findings.
